WEBVTT

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.110
MATT: Dude, why are we here?

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:17.740
SETH: Extra credit.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.490
TIM: I don't even know what a debate is.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.730
SAM: It's where two people argue and nobody changes their mind.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.410
TIM: So like the group chat.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.920
SAM: Exactly like the group chat.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:36.370
ANNOUNCER: Ladies and gentlemen, your moderator...

00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:48.990
ANNOUNCER: ...RICHARD POWERS!

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:51.280
MATT: RICHARD! RICHARD!

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.320
TIM: I LOVE YOU, RICHARD!

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:01.100
SETH: He's a legend, bro. Everybody knows Richard.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:06.090
SAM: The janitor has a Richard poster in his closet.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:17.530
RICHARD: Thank you, thank you. Each candidate gets two minutes. I have a timer, a bell, and a Taser I'm not legally allowed to use.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.790
ANNOUNCER: Our first candidate...

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:20.840
ANNOUNCER: ...HENRY REYES!

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:24.280
SAM: Look at him. Not a hair out of place.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.890
SETH: That's called presidential, Sam.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.490
ANNOUNCER: And his opponent...

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:33.330
ANNOUNCER: ...CLARA POWERS.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.360
CLARA: Hello, everyone! Thank you!

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:43.380
MATT: BOOOOO!

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:45.100
TIM: I don't even know why!

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:48.170
SETH: Instinct, Tim. It's instinct.

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:55.130
CLARA: Hi, sweetie! Mommy's so proud of you!

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.990
MATT: Wait. That's RICHARD'S MOM?

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.050
SAM: How did nobody know this?

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.450
RICHARD: Mrs. Powers, please. We're live.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.213
CLARA: You were such a cute baby, Richard.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:08.780
RICHARD: Mrs. Powers.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.000
CLARA: I changed your diapers.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.640
RICHARD: Mrs. POWERS.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:26.010
RICHARD: First question. Insulin costs. People are paying hundreds of dollars a month just to stay alive. Mrs. Powers, you first.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:36.360
CLARA: Thank you, Richard. Insulin is an issue that is so important to me, and by "me" I mean the voters, and by "voters" I mean you, specifically. Hi.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.110
TIM: She's looking at us like we're a bag of money.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:52.660
CLARA: And before I get into my plan, I'd like to give some historical context. In 1921, insulin was discovered in Toronto...

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:59.150
CLARA: ...which brings me to the history of Canada, a country with many lakes...

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:05.883
CLARA: ...and I think we can all agree that a vial of insulin is, at its core, a metaphor.

00:03:05.933 --> 00:03:12.360
CLARA: I'm so glad you brought that up, because it leads into my five-point framework. Point one...

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.680
RICHARD: Mrs. Powers, that's the bell.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:29.230
CLARA: I'm aware of the bell, Richard. I'm the one who taught you what a bell is. Point one has a subsection, point one-A, which has its own subsection, which I'll call point one-A-prime...

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:42.920
SAM: She still hasn't said what she'd actually do about insulin.

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:48.460
TIM: She said "insulin" four times. Each time she was just looking at us.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:54.780
MATT: She's saying it like a password. "Insulin. Insulin. Please vote for me."

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:58.600
JANITOR: She was on this same sentence when I started here.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.410
SETH: You started here in 1987.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:02.900
JANITOR: I know.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:08.110
RICHARD: Thank you, Mrs. Powers. Henry, your response?

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:16.570
HENRY: I'm capping it at thirty-five dollars a month. I've already met with the pharmacies. Starts Monday.

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:20.310
TIM: He has a date. He gave us a DATE.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:26.000
SETH: And a price! A real number! Like an adult!

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:34.390
MATT: I'd die for that man. Wait, no, I'd live for him, because I'd be able to afford it.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:38.600
CLARA: That's not a real answer! Where's the framework?

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:40.317
HENRY: It's thirty-five dollars.

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:42.790
CLARA: There are implications!

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:44.930
HENRY: Yeah. People don't die.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.140
MATT: THIRTY-FIVE! THIRTY-FIVE! THIRTY-FIVE!

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.250
SAM: Matt, sit down.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:06.280
RICHARD: Mrs. Powers, can you explain how you'd actually pay for your insulin plan?

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:15.860
CLARA: Great question. I'd like to start by saying there is a Marxist approach to the economy.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:19.860
TIM: Did she just say Marxist?

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:23.250
CLARA: You heard me correctly.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:28.340
SETH: She doubled down. Nobody asked her to double down.

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:32.600
MATT: She doubled down on live television, in KANSAS.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:35.820
HENRY: We're talking about insulin.

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:43.510
CLARA: And I care deeply about that, and I've cared about it for a very long time. Since this morning.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:47.220
SAM: Did she just say "since this morning"?

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:51.270
CLARA: I mean, since forever. I have a binder.

00:05:53.090 --> 00:06:00.310
CLARA: Insulin is covered in Section 47, in the appendix, which is currently pending.

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:01.670
HENRY: Pending what?

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:03.780
CLARA: Further study.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:06.960
HENRY: How long?

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:10.417
CLARA: Historically? Decades.

00:06:10.467 --> 00:06:13.160
SETH: She said "decades" like it's a feature.

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:16.710
MATT: Meanwhile people at the pharmacy are doing math on their fingers.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:22.360
CLARA: Which is why I'm proposing a committee to form a task force to explore a roundtable.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:23.680
HENRY: So, no.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.790
CLARA: It's a process, Henry.

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:30.840
HENRY: Insulin's not a process. It's a bottle.

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:34.080
TIM: YOU CAN'T STUDY A BOTTLE, CLARA!

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:37.220
RICHARD: Mrs. Powers, please stop talking.

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:49.520
CLARA: Richard, I did not carry you for nine months to be disrespected on live television.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:53.570
MATT: Nine months. Respect.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:07:01.560
MATT: Mr. Reyes! You actually care about this stuff, right? Like for real?

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:10.450
HENRY: My uncle rations his insulin. I'm not gonna talk about it for twenty minutes and forget about it. I'm gonna fix it.

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:12.870
SAM: Okay, I'm not crying, you're crying.

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:14.360
TIM: I'm not.

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:21.270
HENRY: I already sat down with the pharmaceutical companies. I showed them the numbers. Three of them signed.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:24.430
SETH: Wait, you already DID it?

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:27.830
HENRY: I don't make promises I can't keep.

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:32.397
MATT: That's the most powerful thing anyone has ever said in this gym.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:40.430
RICHARD: Final question. Why should people vote for you? Mrs. Powers, one minute.

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:46.300
CLARA: Thank you. I'd like to start by thanking my own mother...

00:07:54.740 --> 00:08:03.280
CLARA: ...and also my third-grade teacher, Ms. Delgado, who taught me to love learning, and her cat...

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:07.690
SETH: CLARA, YOUR TIME ENDED BEFORE I HIT PUBERTY!

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:10.360
CLARA: ...and the cat's name was Biscuit.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:12.660
RICHARD: Henry?

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:24.740
HENRY: Vote for me, and insulin's thirty-five bucks. Not someday. Monday. That's it. That's the speech.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:30.730
RICHARD: I'm sorry, Mrs. Powers. He had a date.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:32.300
CLARA: RICHARD!

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:36.900
MATT: Henry won in a landslide.

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:38.890
TIM: Ninety-nine percent.

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:42.460
SETH: Who was the one percent?

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:46.310
SAM: Clara. And Biscuit.

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:49.570
SETH: Biscuit's a cat. And also dead.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:53.580
SAM: It's a very tight race.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:09:01.530
CLARA: ...and that, in essence, is why insulin is a metaphor. Now, before I get to point two...

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:04.100
RICHARD: Goodnight, Mrs. Powers.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:09.600
CLARA: ...point one-A-prime...

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:15.500
CLARA: I know I'm going to lose.
